As expected, we can observe the expansion-based solvers outclass the search-based solvers \depqbf and \yasol as well as \zthree. \qfunmini and \qfuncms basically have the same performance, while our approach has the most constant behavior, outperforming all solvers for $n\geq 22$. 

For even larger values of $n$, \qfunmini quickly reaches its limit, incapable of solving these instances before timeout, while \qfuncms still can solve such instances easily. In particular, for $n=100$, no instance was solved by \qfunmini before the timeout. This is likely due to the special handling of XOR formulations by \cms, that \cadical lacks. Hence, for larger values of $n$ we restrict our comparison to \qfuncms and \rqis, as shown in Table~\ref{tab::QRPLargeN}.
