\section{Conclusion}
We introduced a perceptually grounded framework for morphing temporal envelopes, informed by empirical studies on human auditory perception. By identifying key perceptual factors-such as the quantity, spacing, and placement of impulses-we codified the principles that underlie natural-sounding morphs. These principles enabled the creation of a large synthetic dataset of envelope morphing examples, which we used to train a twin network for generating perceptually valid interpolations. Our experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework consistently outperforms conventional methods across multiple benchmarks. Increasingly, modern sound synthesis and generative models are conditioned on temporal envelopes \cite{chung2024t, garcia2025sketch2sound, gramaccioni2024stable, lee2024video}. By providing a perceptually intuitive way to morph these envelopes, our framework can enhance the controllability and naturalness of the resulting sound morphs in such systems. By bridging psycho-acoustics and DL, this work advances audio generation systems toward perceptually grounded morphing - a critical capability with applications in assistive sound design, immersive media, and computational hearing research.

% This work establishes a framework for perceptually valid temporal envelope morphing through systematic integration of psycho-acoustic principles and machine learning. Our experiments demonstrate that standard autoencoding architectures, while effective at reconstructing audio dynamics, fail to preserve perceptual rules during latent space interpolation. By contrast, our rule-aware approach—combining synthetic data with controlled parameter variations and a twin morphing network. By bridging psycho-acoustics and deep learning, this work advances audio generation systems toward perceptually grounded morphing - a critical capability for applications in assistive sound design, immersive media, and computational hearing research. 

