\paragraph{Correlated noise should be injected after upsampling.}
% Cascaded diffusion models with training~\cite{teng2023relay, jin2024pyramidal}, which generate high-resolution images starting from low resolution by training a single model, have focused on the change in noise levels after upsampling.
Cascaded diffusion models trained to generate high-resolution images from low-resolution inputs~\cite{teng2023relay, jin2024pyramidal} have focused on changes in noise levels after upsampling.
% signal-to-noise level ratio (SNR) after upsampling.
% Similar to ~\cite{teng2023relay}, 
Specifically, Pyramidal Flow Matching~\cite{jin2024pyramidal} addressed this problem with correlated noise within flow matching models. Starting from an initial noise $\mathbf{x}_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I})$, flow matching aims to determine the target $\mathbf{x}_1$ by following Eq.~\eqref{eq:rectified_flow} through a denoising process. Then, The conditional distribution of $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_t$ at timestep $t$ is:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathbf{x}}_t | \mathbf{x}_1\sim \mathcal{N}\left(t\mathbf{x}_1, (1-t)^2 \mathbf{I}\right).
\end{equation}
But after upsampling, the distribution of the upsampled latent becomes:
\begin{equation}
\text{Up}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}) | \mathbf{x}_1 \sim \mathcal{N}\left(t\text{Up}(\mathbf{x}_1), (1-{t})^2 \mathbf{\Sigma}\right),
\label{eq:upsampled_latent_distribution}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{\Sigma}$ is the upsampling covariance matrix, and $\text{Up}(\cdot)$ denotes the upsampling function. Since $\mathbf{\Sigma}$ is not proportional to the identity matrix regardless of the type of upsampling, the conditional distribution of $\text{Up}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})$ cannot be on the trajectory Eq.~\eqref{eq:rectified_flow}. Therefore, correlated noise should be injected to enforce an isotropic covariance and bring the latent back to the original trajectory.

% \paragraph{Timestep rescheduling in training-free methods}
\paragraph{Noise injection in a training-free manner causes noise-timestep mismatch.}
Since adding correlated noise shifts the timestep towards the noise, a modified timestep scheduling is required. Unified training models~\cite{teng2023relay, jin2024pyramidal} become fitted to the timestep scheduling used during training.
% But in a training-free manner, using the same timestep scheduling as pretrained model leads to being sampled more frequently at certain intervals.
However, in a training-free setting, using the same timestep schedule as the pretrained model leads to certain intervals being oversampled.
% For example, consider a 2-stage cascade structure that first denoises at $[0, t_1]$ at low resolution, then upsamples and adds noise, followed by denoising from at $[t_2, 1]$. Since $t2 < t1$, $[t_2, t_1]$ interval is sampled twice.
If the interval is closer to noise, it will generate relatively more low-frequency information; if closer to the image, more high-frequency information. In any case, this results in frequency imbalance, leading to noise-timestep mismatch artifacts, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:two_artifacts}(b).

Therefore, to prevent mismatch artifacts in the training-free method, it is critical to preserve the original model's timestep distribution, which defines how frequently different noise levels (timesteps) are sampled during generation~\cite{zheng2024beta}.
We address this by proposing Noise-Timestep rescheduling with Distribution Matching (NT-DM) in \S\ref{subsec:NT-rescheduling}.

