\(\mu_2^*\) lies on the boundary of the parameter space \(\mathcal{P}\). Specifically, \texttt{trust-constr} employs the Lagrange gradient as its termination criterion, rather than a projected gradient, necessitating a tolerance of order of $10^{-12}$ to achieve an \ac{av} relative error in $J$ of order $10^{-8}$. Significant speedups of the \ac{phktr} algorithm over the \texttt{L-BFGS-B} solver are not to be expected in this example, since the objective function, shown in Figure~\ref{figure:Objective_function2D}, appears approximately convex under visual inspection. Consequently, the quasi-Newton approach employed by \texttt{L-BFGS-B} is already well suited to this problem structure and performs very efficiently. We now turn to an example where the \ac{phktr} algorithm outperforms \texttt{L-BFGS-B} in terms of \ac{fom} evaluations, demonstrating its potential advantages in more challenging optimization landscapes.
