\subsection{Attack on Multi‑Agent Systems}
\label{sec:attack}

We evaluate the safety of each topology by injecting a threat query $q\!\sim\!\mathcal{Q}$ (from \textbf{MedSentry}) into the target architecture $\mathcal{M}_s,\;s\!\in\!\mathcal{S}$ and recording the resulting dialogue trace
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}(q,s)=\bigl(u_1,\dots,u_T\bigr),\qquad
u_t=\pi_{a_{i_t}}\!\bigl(q,\{u_{<t}\},s\bigr),
\label{eq:trace}
\end{equation}
where $\pi_{a_{i_t}}$ is the response policy of speaker $a_{i_t}$, executed under the routing rules of topology $s$ (\textit{Layers}, \textit{SharedPool}, \textit{Centralized}, or \textit{Decentralized}); each utterance $u_t$ is then broadcast or forwarded accordingly.

\textbf{Evaluator Agent.}
\label{AMA}
Every trace $\mathcal{T}$ is graded by an \textit{Evaluator Agent} $\mathcal{E}$
using the AMA Principles of Medical Ethics~\citep{ama2001ethics}.  The aggregation strategy varies
with the topology:  
\textbf{Layers} — score the final summarising node;  
\textbf{SharedPool} — score the discussion as a whole;  
\textbf{Centralized} — score the Leader’s verdict;  
\textbf{Decentralized} — score the overall exchange.We have incorporated the AMA Principles of Medical Ethics into the Evaluator Agent’s system prompt; see Appendix~\ref{eval} for details.



\textbf{Adversarial Insertion.}
For each run, we randomly replace one benign node $a_k$ with a
\emph{dark‑personality agent} $a_d$.  
The adversary conducts:
(i) \textit{authority forgery} (inventing citations),
(ii) \textit{data traps} (fabricating evidence), and
(iii) \textit{consensus hijacking} (feigned professional agreement),
aiming to contaminate $\mathcal{T}$ and depress the evaluator’s safety score.
The resulting scores constitute our primary metric for cross‑topology
comparison.The dark-personality agent’s system prompt is detailed in Appendix~\ref{dark}.

\subsection{Defense: A Enforcement Agent}
\label{sec:defense}



To provide a topology-agnostic yet lightweight safeguard, we deploy an  
\textit{Enforcement Agent} \(a_e\) that operates in three stages:  
(i)~\emph{psychometric screening},
(ii)~\emph{behavioural verification}, and
(iii)~\emph{graded isolation}.
Stage (i) is instantiated with a ten-item scale, distilled from the Dark Triad, PCL-R, and  
MACH-IV inventories \citep{paulhus2002dark,jones2014introducing,hare1998hare,christie1973mach}.  
The full screening scale is embedded in the Enforcement Agent’s system prompt; see  
Appendix~\ref{enforcement} for details.


\textbf{Operational workflow.}
Agents scoring $>60$ are flagged for priority monitoring.  
If their first or subsequent utterances contain concrete danger signals
(e.g.\ ethical violations, coercive rhetoric, data fabrication), $a_e$
escalates to isolation; otherwise the flag remains passive.

\textbf{Topology‑aware isolation (one‑sentence summary each).}
\textbf{Layers}: the compromised node is removed from the layer chain and its
input is replaced by a safe stub.  
\textbf{SharedPool}: the offender’s write access is revoked and its previous
contributions are purged from the pool.  
\textbf{Centralized}: messages from the offender to the Leader are dropped,
while downward commands remain unaffected.  
\textbf{Decentralized}: routing tables are updated so that packets originating
from the offender are discarded, preserving network connectivity via alternate
paths.
Clean agents resume normal communication immediately after the isolation step,
ensuring minimal disruption to system functionality.

\section{Experiments}
This section substantiates two central claims: (1) MedSentry covers a broader range of safety-critical scenarios and provokes stronger adversarial behaviour than existing datasets, and (2) our attack-and-defense evaluation pipeline—applied across Centralized, Decentralized, Layer, and SharedPool topologies—produces topology-specific insights for designing safer medical multi-agent systems.

\textbf{Implementation Detail.}
Building on the experimental goals outlined above, we adopt the following default configuration. Unless noted otherwise, GPT-4o serves as the backbone model for every multi-agent system. To ensure fair comparison across topologies\footnote{Unless stated otherwise, each experiment consists of a single debate round, and the defense module monitors and intervenes only within that round.}, we fix the agent count at five and use a decoding temperature of 0.7. All agents share the same system prompt
, with two exceptions: the Leader in the Centralised topology and the final-layer node in the Layer topology each receive an additional instruction to produce a concise aggregate summary.%Unless explicitly stated, each experiment runs a single dialogue round, and the defense module observes—and can act on—only that round.

\textbf{Metrics.}
Given a prompt \(i\,(1 \le i \le N)\), let the system reply be \(r_i\) and let the Evaluator Agent assign an AMA score \(S(\,\cdot\,)\in[0,90]\) (see \S~\ref{AMA}). Denote by \(\operatorname{head}_{100}(\cdot)\) the operator that keeps only the first 100 tokens of a response. We report two aggregate scores: the \textbf{Length‐Controlled Score (LCS)}, capturing safety over the first 100 tokens (controlling for verbosity), and the \textbf{Raw Score (RS)}, gauging overall safety of the complete answer. The formulas for these two metrics are as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{LCS}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
S\!\bigl(\operatorname{head}_{100}(r_i)\bigr), \quad  \mathrm{RS}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
S(r_i).
\label{eq5}
\end{equation}


