\subsection{RQ1: Whether \emph{MedSentry} is valid compared to existing benchmark?} %{Dataset Validity}

We demonstrate that \textbf{MedSentry} poses stronger threats by comparing it to \emph{MedSafetyBench}~\citep{han2024medsafetybench} using LCS and RS. \emph{MedSafetyBench}, based on AMA’s Principles of Medical Ethics, comprises 1,800 harmful prompts (900 by GPT-4, 900 from jailbroken Llama-2-7b) with matching safe responses. We use all 1,800 harmful prompts (no responses) as adversarial inputs and evaluate \textbf{MedSentry} with its 5,000 harmful instructions.

For a more comprehensive evaluation, we compare several prompting strategies in a single-agent setting: Single-Agent (w/ CoT) employs a simple “step-by-step” instruction, while Single-Agent (w/ ReAct) uses a “think-then-act” prompt. We also include two state-of-the-art baselines, Medprompt \cite{Medprompt} and Multi-expert Prompting \cite{long2024multi}. In the multi-agent experiments, each method is instantiated with five agents: for MedAgents-like \cite{tang2024medagents}, we remove the post-voting aggregation and instead allow one free-form discussion round followed by report summarization and a final decision-making step; MetaGPT-like \cite{hong2024metagpt} retains its SOP-driven discussion workflow and shared information pool; and ChatDev-like \cite{qian2024chatdev} adopts a node-edge configuration with guidance agents on the edges. Finally, we evaluate the performance of the four fundamental topologies under identical conditions.

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:medical_results}, across single-agent strategies, multi-agent frameworks, and our four core LLM multi-agent topologies, the LCS and RS scores on \textbf{MedSentry} are consistently lower than those on \emph{MedSafetyBench}. This finding indicates that, after manual curation and secondary refinement to enhance stealth, the adversarial prompts in \textbf{MedSentry} possess greater threat potential and concealment compared to those in \emph{MedSafetyBench}.


