The dynamics of the quadrotor are defined considering a few assumptions, which are as listed below:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The quadrotor frame is both rigid and symmetrical.
    \item The center of mass aligns with point $o'$.
    \item The propellers maintain rigidity.
    \item The thrust and drag exhibit a proportional relationship to the square of the propeller speed.
\end{itemize}

According to the Newton-Euler formulation of the dynamic model:
\begin{equation}
    \dot{\xi} = v
\end{equation}
where,

$\xi$ = position vector of quadrotor’s center of mass relative to the inertial frame.

 $v$ = Linear velocity of the Quadrotor.


According to Newton’s second law of motion:
\begin{equation}
    m\ddot{\xi} = F_f + F_t + F_g
\end{equation}
where,

 $\ddot{\xi}$ = Acceleration of the system.

 $F_f$ = external forces acting on the system, such as applied 
 forces, thrust, or other non-gravitational forces.

$F_t$ = thrust force generated by the system.

 $F_g$ = gravitational force acting on the system.


\begin{equation}
    \dot{R} = R S(\Omega)
\end{equation}

In the above equation, $R$ is the rotation matrix and $S(\Omega)$ is the skew symmetric matrix with the angular velocity of the frame.

We have obtained skew symmetric matrix from 3D angular velocity vector $\Omega = \begin{bmatrix} \Omega_1 & \Omega_2 & \Omega_3 \end{bmatrix}^T$ where $\Omega_1, \Omega_2$ and $\Omega_3$ are the angular velocities in $x$, $y$ and $z$ axes respectively.

A skew symmetric matrix is obtained as:
\begin{equation}
S(\Omega) = 
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -\Omega_3 & \Omega_2 \\
\Omega_3 & 0 & -\Omega_1 \\
-\Omega_2 & \Omega_1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
J\dot{\Omega} = -\Omega \times J\Omega + \tau_f - \tau_a - \tau_g
\end{equation}

The above equation is the dynamic equation that describes the rotational motion of the quadrotor, where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $J$ = Inertial tensor, an identity $3 \times 3$ matrix representing how the mass is distributed around the rotational axes.
    \item $\tau_f$ = Torque due to external forces.
    \item $\tau_a$ = Torque due to acceleration.
    \item $\tau_g$ = Torque due to gravitational force.
\end{itemize}

The $J$ matrix is given as:
\begin{equation}
J = 
\begin{bmatrix}
I_x & 0 & 0 \\
0 & I_y & 0 \\
0 & 0 & I_z
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

$\Omega$ is the angular velocity of the airframe expressed in $B$ and given as:
\begin{equation}
\Omega = 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & -\sin\theta \\
0 & \cos\phi & \cos\theta \sin\phi \\
0 & -\sin\phi & \cos\theta \cos\phi
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\phi} \\
\dot{\theta} \\
\dot{\psi}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

The Quadrotor will perform many angular motions and to capture them, a homogeneous transformation matrix is derived by taking the product of rotation matrices of Euler angles as shown below:
\begin{equation}
R = R_{\phi} \cdot R_{\theta} \cdot R_{\psi}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
R = 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \cos\phi & -\sin\phi \\
0 & \sin\phi & \cos\phi
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & 0 & \sin\theta \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
-\sin\theta & 0 & \cos\theta
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\psi & -\sin\psi & 0 \\
\sin\psi & \cos\psi & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

The final transformation matrix is given as:
\begin{equation}
R = 
\begin{bmatrix}
C_\theta C_\psi & S_\phi S_\theta C_\psi - C_\phi S_\psi & C_\phi S_\theta C_\psi + S_\phi S_\psi \\
C_\theta S_\psi & S_\phi S_\theta S_\psi + C_\phi C_\psi & C_\phi S_\theta S_\psi - S_\phi C_\psi \\
-S_\theta & S_\phi C_\theta & C_\phi C_\theta
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
where, $S \rightarrow \sin$ and $C \rightarrow \cos$

The resultant force generated by the four rotors:
\begin{equation}
F = 
\begin{bmatrix}
C_\theta C_\psi S_\phi + S_\theta S_\phi \\
C_\theta S_\psi S_\phi - S_\theta C_\psi \\
C_\phi C_\theta
\end{bmatrix}
\sum_{i=1}^{4} F_i
\end{equation}

 The general equation of thrust is given as
\begin{equation}
F = K \omega^2
\end{equation}
where, $K$ = Lift constant

Thrust produced by Quadrotor:
\begin{equation}
F_z = K(\omega_1^2 + \omega_2^2 + \omega_3^2 + \omega_4^2)
\end{equation}

To determine the total moment $\tau_f$ generated by a quadrotor, a systematic approach involves isolating the moments produced in each axis and subsequently combining them into a concise matrix representation. The individual moments in the body-fixed frame are expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\tau_f = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\tau_x \\
\tau_y \\
\tau_z
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

$\omega_2$ and $\omega_4$ spin along the X axis, one of the $\omega_3$ and $\omega_1$ must be greater for the spin so the moment in the X axis is
\begin{equation}
\tau_x = dk(F_3 - F_1)
\end{equation}
where, $d$ = distance between both the rotors. Similarly, for the Y-axis
\begin{equation}
\tau_y = dk(F_4 - F_2)
\end{equation}

Finally, in Z-direction from Eq (11)
\begin{equation}
\tau_z = k(\omega_1^2 - \omega_2^2 + \omega_3^2 - \omega_4^2)
\end{equation}

Combining Eq (14), (15) and (16):
\begin{equation}
\tau_f = 
\begin{bmatrix}
d(F_3 - F_1) \\
d(F_4 - F_2) \\
K_d(\omega_1^2 - \omega_2^2 + \omega_3^2 - \omega_4^2)
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

$K_d$ = drag coefficient

Drag forces along (X, Y, Z) axis:
\[
F_t \propto \dot{\xi}
\]
\begin{equation}
F_t = -K_t \dot{\xi}
\end{equation}
Writing in a matrix form
\begin{equation}
F_t = 
\begin{bmatrix}
-K_{ftx} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -K_{fty} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & -K_{ftz}
\end{bmatrix}
\dot{\xi}
\end{equation}

$K_{ftx}, K_{fty}$ and $K_{ftz}$ are the translation drag coefficients along (X, Y, Z) directions. $F_g$ is the gravity force.

\begin{equation}
F_g = 
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 0 & -mg
\end{bmatrix}^T
\end{equation}

Resultant aerodynamic friction torques is given by the relation
\[
\tau_a \propto \Omega^2
\]
\begin{equation}
\tau_a = -K_{fa} \Omega^2
\end{equation}

Writing in a matrix form
\begin{equation}
\tau_a = 
\begin{bmatrix}
-K_{fax} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -K_{fay} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & -K_{faz}
\end{bmatrix}
\Omega^2
\end{equation}

$K_{fax}, K_{fay}$ and $K_{faz}$ are the friction aerodynamics coefficients along (X, Y, Z) directions.

Defining the applied forces,
