\subsection{External data validation}\label{external-data-validation}

As a first illustration of the approach, Fig. 1 shows multidimensional scaling (MDS) visualizations of the \texttt{SPECTER} cosine similarities between all papers of matched treatment and control author pairs for six randomly chosen pairs. Note that control papers (labeled ``C'') are typically grouped somewhat closer together than those of matched comparable CDF funded researchers (``T''), which lends initial qualitative support to our approach. A visualization of the complete validation data set of all 86 author pairs is shown in Fig. 3, where paper position symbols are also scaled by approximate author contribution as inferred from author position and co-author count of papers. Symbol size is proportional to harmonic co-author credit scores (\citeproc{ref-hagen2008harmonic}{Hagen, 2008}; \citeproc{ref-hodge1981publication}{Hodge \& Greenberg, 1981}).

Fig. \ref{fig:group-avg-comp} shows the author average cosine similarities of papers of matched treatment-control pairs. The similarities between papers are larger on average for the control group (mean: 0.67, sd: 0.06) as a whole compared to the treatment group (mean: 0.63, sd: 0.06). For the majority of pairs (72 \%), the value for the control group is also greater than that of the treatment group. This provides a fundamental confirmation that the text embeddings similarity approach in the knowledge space framework works as expected and that the most simple baseline candidate measure is able to measure epistemic breadth to some degree.

