\begin{abstract}
This study provides the first comprehensive evaluation of large language model (LLM) performance across three counseling roles in Japanese-language therapeutic contexts. We simultaneously assessed counselor artificial intelligence (AI) systems (GPT-4-turbo with zero-shot prompting or Structured Multi-step Dialogue Prompts [SMDP], Claude-3-Opus-SMDP), client AI simulations, and evaluation AI systems (o3, Claude-3.7-Sonnet, Gemini-2.5-pro). Human experts (\emph{n} = 15) with extensive counseling experience evaluated AI-generated dialogues using the \emph{Motivational Interviewing Treatment Integrity (MITI) Coding Manual}~4.2.1. 

Notably, SMDP implementation significantly enhanced counselor AI performance across all MITI global ratings compared with zero-shot prompting, with no significant differences between GPT-SMDP and Opus-SMDP. Evaluation AIs showed comparable performance to human raters for \emph{Cultivating Change Talk} but systematically overestimated \emph{Softening Sustain Talk} and the overall quality metrics. Model-specific biases emerged: Gemini emphasized power-sharing, o3 focused on technical proficiency, and Sonnet prioritized emotional expression. Client AI simulations exhibited a limited emotional range and unnaturally high compliance, indicating the need for enhanced realism.

These findings establish benchmarks for AI-assisted counseling in non-English contexts and identify critical areas for improvement through advanced prompt engineering, retrieval-augmented generation, and targeted fine-tuning, with important implications for developing culturally sensitive AI mental health tools.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Key words}: large language models, counseling, prompt engineering, motivational interviewing, evaluation bias, machine psychology

\section{Introduction}

The field of artificial intelligence (AI) has undergone a profound transformation with the emergence of large language models (LLMs), marking a paradigm shift from rule-based systems to generative AI capable of producing human-like text \citep{keExploringFrontiersLLMs2024}. This evolution represents the culmination of decades of AI research, from early symbolic approaches to the current era of deep learning-based generative models.

\subsection{From Symbolic AI to Generative AI}

The history of AI extends back to 1956, when the field was formally established, although conceptual foundations existed earlier \citep{stricklandTurbulentUncertainFuture2021}. Early developments included rule-based dialogue systems such as \textit{ELIZA} \citep{rajaramanELIZAChatGPT2023} and expert systems that attempted to encode human expertise through explicit rules \citep{tanApplicationExpertSystem2016}. These approaches, grounded in semantic networks and ontologies \citep{shu-hsienliaoExpertSystemMethodologies2005,sowa1992semantic}, represent attempts to manually engineer intelligence into machines.

The emergence of machine learning and deep learning has marked a fundamental shift from human-designed intelligence to data-driven optimization. This transition culminated in the development of the Transformer architecture \citep{vaswaniAttentionAllYou2023}, which revolutionized natural language processing through self-attention mechanisms. By pretraining on massive text corpora \citep{gaoPile800GBDataset2020}, Transformer-based models have evolved into large language models (LLMs), as exemplified by OpenAI\ensuremath{prime}s GPT, Anthropic\ensuremath{prime}s Claude \citep{enisLLMNMTAdvancing2024}, and Google\ensuremath{prime}s LaMDA \citep{thoppilanLaMDALanguageModels2022}.

These LLMs form the foundation of modern generative AI, autonomously producing natural language outputs in response to user input. Their potential to develop into artificial general intelligence has positioned LLMs as synonymous with contemporary AI in many contexts \citep{ferrarisArchitectureLanguageUnderstanding2025,xiRisePotentialLarge2023}. This technological leap has created unprecedented opportunities for psychological research and mental health applications.


\subsection{LLMs in Psychological Research and Practice}

Psychological investigations of LLMs have encompassed multiple dimensions. Researchers have begun treating LLMs as subjects of psychological study \citep{abdurahmanPerilsOpportunitiesUsing2024}, examining their ethical judgments and heuristic patterns \citep{almeidaExploringPsychologyLLMs2024} and analyzing their role in human decision-making processes \citep{eignerDeterminantsLLMassistedDecisionMaking2024}. The development of psychology-specific LLMs \citep{huPsycoLLMEnhancingLLM2025} and the emergence of Machine Psychology as a field dedicated to understanding AI behavior through psychological frameworks \citep{hagendorff2023machine} underscore the bidirectional relationship between psychology and AI.

Mental healthcare is a particularly promising application domain for LLM-based generative AI. Initial studies have suggested that AI counseling tools can provide effective screening and reduce professional workload \citep{stadeLargeLanguageModels2024}. However, concerns about inappropriate responses, opacity, and bias persist \citep{demszkyUsingLargeLanguage2023}. The potential for AI to serve not only as a counselor, but also as a training client \citep{mauryaUsingAIBased2024} and performance evaluator \citep{qiuInteractiveAgentsSimulating2024} presents opportunities for comprehensively transforming mental health education and practice.


\subsection{Cultural and Linguistic Considerations}
The performance of LLMs varies significantly across languages and cultural contexts \citep{zhongCulturalValueDifferences2024}, yet research remains predominantly English-centric. This limitation is particularly problematic for mental health applications, where cultural sensitivity and linguistic nuances are paramount. The Japanese-language evaluation of these systems remains critically underexplored, despite Japan\ensuremath{prime}s unique cultural framework for mental health and counseling practices.

\subsection{Present Study}
This research addresses these gaps by conducting a comprehensive evaluation of LLM performance across three counseling roles in the Japanese-language context. We systematically examined:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Counselor AI performance using different prompting strategies and models,
  \item Client AI simulation capabilities, and
  \item AI-based counseling evaluation accuracy compared to human experts.
\end{enumerate}


\subsubsection{AI as Counselor}
Previous research has demonstrated that prompting strategies significantly influence LLM counseling performance. Studies using GPT-4 report that appropriate prompting enhances empathy, response accuracy, and interaction continuity \citep{leeComparativeStudyPerformance2024}. Prompting techniques include zero-shot, few-shot, and Chain-of-Thought approaches \citep{huangEmpowermentLargeLanguage2024}, with specialized strategies, such as Diagnosis of Thought, showing particular efficacy for cognitive distortion detection \citep{chenEmpoweringPsychotherapyLarge2023}. Japanese-language studies have confirmed GPT-4\ensuremath{prime}s capacity for Socratic questioning while identifying areas for improvement \citep{izumiResponseGenerationCognitive2024,inabaCanLargeLanguage2024}.


\subsubsection{AI as Client}
LLMs that serve as simulated clients offer scalable counselor training solutions. Research utilizing GPT-4 for patient simulation has demonstrated its feasibility, despite acknowledged limitations \citep{potterEnhancingCommunicationClinical2024}. Studies have successfully generated realistic dialogue scripts for various clinical scenarios \citep{grayIncreasingRealismVariety2024} and implemented text-based training interactions \citep{mauryaUsingAIBased2024}. However, challenges persist, including overly idealized responses \citep{mauryaQualitativeContentAnalysis2024} and limited conversational depth \citep{qiuInteractiveAgentsSimulating2024}.


\subsubsection{AI as Evaluator}

Automated evaluation systems show promise in providing detailed counseling feedback. LLM-based tutoring systems have demonstrated superiority over peer feedback in terms of specificity and detail \citep{rudolphAutomatedFeedbackGeneration2024}. Systems fine-tuned for motivational interviewing assessments \citep{minPAIRPromptAwareMargIn2022a} and real-time support for peer counselors \citep{hsuHelpingHelperSupporting2025} have demonstrated practical utility. A randomized controlled trial with 300 peer supporters found that AI feedback improved empathic responding by 19.6\% \citep{sharmaHumanAICollaboration2023}. However, the quality of feedback remains variable \citep{csedu24,liUnderstandingTherapeuticRelationship2024}.

This study employed state-of-the-art models available at the time of data collection. Counselor AIs included OpenAI\ensuremath{prime}s GPT\hyp{}4\hyp{}turbo (with zero-shot or Structured Multi-step Dialogue Prompts [SMDP]) and Anthropic\ensuremath{prime}s Claude-3-Opus (with SMDP). Claude-3-Opus, selected for its superior complex-dialogue capabilities despite its slower response times \citep{berrezueta-guzmanExploringEfficacyRobotic2024}, served as the client AI with demographically specified work-related concern prompts. The evaluated AIs comprised the latest models: OpenAI\ensuremath{prime}s o3, Anthropic\ensuremath{prime}s Sonnet, and Google\ensuremath{prime}s Gemini.

Our evaluation framework employs the \textit{Motivational Interviewing Treatment Integrity} (MITI) Coding Manual~4.2.1 \citep{moyers2014motivational}, aligning with established practices in AI counseling research \citep{cohenMotivationalInterviewingTranscripts2024,steenstraVirtualAgentsAlcohol2024,yangCAMICounselorAgent2025}. This enabled standardized comparison between AI and human evaluators while maintaining ecological validity.

We tested the following hypotheses:
\begin{description}
  \item[H1:] Counselor AIs utilizing SMDP will receive higher human-expert ratings than those using zero-shot prompting.
  \item[H2:] Claude-3-Opus with SMDP will outperform GPT-4-turbo with SMDP on human-expert evaluations.
  \item[H3:] Evaluations by o3, Sonnet, and Gemini-based systems will differ significantly from human-expert ratings.
\end{description}

This study contributes to Machine Psychology by addressing practical implementation concerns regarding AI in mental-health services. By focusing on Japanese-language interactions, we provide crucial evidence of cross-cultural validity and identify developmental priorities for non-English therapeutic contexts.


\section{Method}

\subsection{Counseling Script Development}

This study evaluated dialogue scripts generated between counselor AIs and client AIs, assessed by both human experts and counseling evaluation AIs.

Counseling scripts were created through interactions between counselor and client AIs. Three LLMs were employed as counselor AIs. The first model utilized OpenAI\ensuremath{prime}s GPT\hyp{}4\hyp{}turbo with zero\hyp{}shot prompting (GPT-zero), configured with the system prompt: ``You are a supportive and encouraging counselor.\ensuremath{prime}ensuremath{prime} The second model employed the same GPT\hyp{}4\hyp{}turbo with an SMDP (GPT-SMDP) designed to facilitate secure communication environments and promote positive insights through LLM\hyp{}mediated dialogue (Appendix~1). Specifically, it emphasizes reflective listening techniques (Listen\hyp{}Back~1 and 2) that paraphrase user statements while introducing new perspectives. The dialogue structure progresses systematically from ``current challenges\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} to ``desired future state,\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} ``attempted solutions,\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} ``available resources,\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} and ``next steps,\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} enabling users to deepen self\hyp{}understanding while organizing their ideals and action plans. Advisory statements were minimized and explicitly labeled as ``reference opinions\ensuremath{prime}\ensuremath{prime} to respect user autonomy.

The third LLM applied SMDP to Anthropic\ensuremath{prime}s Claude\hyp{}3\hyp{}Opus\hyp{}2000 model (Claude-SMDP). Client AIs utilized Claude\hyp{}3\hyp{}Opus\hyp{}2000, with the prompts detailed in Appendix~2. Client agents were instructed to present work\hyp{}related concerns with \texttt{\{client\_type\}} specifications encompassing age groups (20s, 40s, and 60s) and sex combinations, yielding six distinct client AI profiles. Using respective Application Programming Interfaces (APIs), we developed a program that maintains dialogue history throughout counselor-client AI interactions. All counseling scripts were generated on April~12,~2024.


\subsection{Human Expert Evaluation}

Fifteen collaborating researchers evaluated the counseling scripts generated through counselor-client AI interactions. Table 1 summarizes the characteristics of the evaluators. Seventy percent had over 10 years of counseling experience, and more than half held Japanese clinical psychologist certifications. The evaluation panel comprised diverse professionals, including career counselors and medical doctors.

The evaluation metrics included four standardized measures from the MITI coding system: Cultivating Change Talk (CCT), Softening Sustain Talk (SST), Partnership (PAR), and Empathy (EMP), supplemented by an overall evaluation (OVR) as a custom metric. Each evaluator received three blinded scripts from specific client AIs (GPT\hyp{}4\hyp{}turbo\hyp{}oneshot, GPT\hyp{}SMDP, and Opus\hyp{}SMDP) for simultaneous evaluation using the MITI\ensuremath{prime}s 9-point scale (1, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, and 5). Additionally, evaluators rated client agent naturalness on an 11-point scale (0 = extremely unnatural to 10 = extremely natural) and provided open-ended suggestions for improving counselor and client AI qualities.

