\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

\subsubsection{Counselor AI Evaluation}

Linear mixed-effects models were used to examine the human ratings of the counselor AI responses. The specified model is as follows:

\begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
  \mathrm{Score}_{ij}
    &= \beta_{0}
     + \beta_{1}\,\mathrm{Model}_{\text{one-shot},ij}
     + \beta_{2}\,\mathrm{Model}_{\text{gpt-smdp},ij} \\
    &\quad
     + \beta_{3}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{40\text{s},ij}
     + \beta_{4}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{60\text{s},ij}
     + \beta_{5}\,\mathrm{Sex}_{\text{male},ij} \\
    &\quad
     + u_{0j} + \varepsilon_{ij}.
  \end{aligned}
  \end{equation}


\noindent
where $u_{0j}$ represents the random intercept for rater $j$. Each of the five indicators was evaluated using separate models. Opus\hyp{}SMDP/20s/female served as the reference level, with $t$-tests conducted using Kenward--Roger/Satterthwaite degrees of freedom. Additionally, three pairwise comparisons (Opus\hyp{}SMDP--GPT\hyp{}zero, Opus\hyp{}SMDP--GPT\hyp{}SMDP, and GPT\hyp{}zero--GPT\hyp{}SMDP) were performed with Tukey--Kramer adjustment.

For the counseling evaluation of AI assessments, linear mixed-effects models were estimated for each score indicator (OVR, CCT, SST, PAR, and EMP), including interactions between counselor models (Opus\hyp{}SMDP/GPT\hyp{}zero/GPT\hyp{}SMDP) and rater groups (human/Sonnet\hyp{}LLM/o3\hyp{}LLM/Gemini\hyp{}LLM):

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Score}_{ijk}
  &= \beta_{0}
   + \sum_{m=1}^{2} \beta_{m}\,\mathrm{Model}_{m,ij}
   + \sum_{g=3}^{5} \beta_{g}\,\mathrm{Group}_{g,ij} \\
  &\quad
   + \sum_{h=6}^{11} \beta_{h}\,(\mathrm{Model}\times\mathrm{Group})_{h,ij} \\
  &\quad
   + \beta_{12}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{40\text{s},ij}
   + \beta_{13}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{60\text{s},ij}
   + \beta_{14}\,\mathrm{Sex}_{ij} \\
  &\quad
   + u^{(\mathrm{human})}_{0j}
   + v^{(\mathrm{AI})}_{0k}
   + \varepsilon_{ijk}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}


\noindent
Random intercepts $u^{(\mathrm{human})}_{0j}$ and $v^{(\mathrm{AI})}_{0k}$ represent the human and AI evaluator clusters, respectively. The estimation employed the ML method with the Satterthwaite approximation. Multiple comparisons were used to examine the main effect of pairwise comparisons across the four rater groups (six comparisons) with Tukey--Kramer adjustment. Exploratory analyses investigated simple interaction effects within the counselor AI models (Opus\hyp{}SMDP/GPT\hyp{}zero/GPT\hyp{}SMDP), with Tukey correction for the same six comparisons, calculating Cohen\ensuremath{prime}s $d$ standardized by the residual SD. The five indicators were treated as theoretically independent, without cross-indicator correction.

\subsubsection{Inter-rater Reliability}

Inter-rater reliability (IRR) was assessed using the \textsf{R} \texttt{psych} package\ensuremath{prime}s intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) function, employing Shrout and Fleiss\ensuremath{prime}s Model 2 framework to estimate the ICC(2,1) (single rater) and ICC(2,\emph{k}) (\emph{k}-rater average) agreements for each indicator and rater group. When the matrices included scripts that were not evaluated by specific raters, the function internally extracted valid pairwise observations for variance component estimation. Interpretation followed Koo \& Li (2016) criteria: \textless0.50 poor, 0.50--0.75 moderate, 0.75--0.90 good, and \textgreater0.90 excellent.

\subsubsection{Sample Size Design and Pre-registration}

Initially, we planned complete data collection with ANOVA and post-hoc analyses. The sample size was calculated using \textsf{G*Power}~3.1 for the most stringent comparison---counseling evaluation of AI\ensuremath{prime}s four levels (including humans). Assuming a medium effect size (0.5), power of 0.95, and a significance level adjusted to 0.025 for Tukey\ensuremath{prime}s method, the required sample size was 22 per two-group comparison. Therefore, we targeted 198 evaluations from 11 evaluators across 18 script types (6 client AI profiles \ensuremath{\times} 3 counselor AIs). However, the final dataset comprises 162 evaluations from 15 evaluators with incomplete data. Post-hoc power analyses were conducted via simulation for both counselor AI and counseling evaluation AI assessments (Appendix~5). All analyses were performed using \textsf{R}~v4.4.1, \textsf{lme4}~v1.1.37, \textsf{emmeans}~v1.11.0, and \textsf{SIMR}~v1.0.7. The research protocol was pre-registered (\url{https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/VU286}).

\subsection{Ethical Considerations}
This study did not involve human participants, animals, or identifiable personal data; therefore, formal ethics committee review was not required.


\section{Results}
\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}
Table 2 presents descriptive statistics for the data collected by each evaluator group. Human experts completed 54 evaluations, while the counseling evaluation AIs conducted 66 evaluations for each model, each including assessments of scripts from three different counselor AI models. Client AI settings were balanced equally across age and gender. Human experts\ensuremath{prime} ratings of client AI naturalness averaged 4.72 \ensuremath{\pm} 2.02 on a 10-point scale, indicating slightly below-midpoint ratings with considerable variability.
Counselor AI evaluations revealed notable differences between human experts and evaluation AIs. While human expert ratings ranged from approximately 2.0 to 3.5 points, counseling evaluation AIs consistently provided higher ratings, with minimum scores around 2.5 points. Notably, Gemini showed particularly high ratings, with some models averaging 5.0 points on the 5-point scale.

