\subsection*{A5.1 Counselor-AI Scores}

\subsubsection*{A5.1.1 Power to Detect Pairwise Differences}

In the counselor-AI analysis, we fixed the fixed-effects coefficients and variance components obtained from the original data and generated 1\,000 Monte-Carlo data sets of size $N=162$ (15 raters), preserving the observed missing-data pattern.  
Each replicate was refitted with the same linear mixed model,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:counselor_model}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Score}_{ij}
  &= \beta_{0}
   + \beta_{1}\,\mathrm{Model}_{\text{gpt-zero},ij}
   + \beta_{2}\,\mathrm{Model}_{\text{gpt-smdp},ij} \\
  &\quad + \beta_{3}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{40s,ij}
   + \beta_{4}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{60s,ij}
   + \beta_{5}\,\mathrm{Sex}_{\text{male},ij} \\
  &\quad + u_{0j}
   + \varepsilon_{ij},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $u_{0j}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{evaluator}}\bigr)$ is a rater-specific random intercept and $\varepsilon_{ij}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon}\bigr)$ is the residual.  
Two-sided $z$ tests with Tukey adjustment were applied to each refit; \emph{power} was defined as the proportion of adjusted $p$-values below .05 across the 1\,000 replicates.

\subsubsection*{A5.1.2 Sample Size for Equivalence (Opus-SMDP vs.\ GPT-SMDP)}

To estimate the sample size required to demonstrate that the OVR scores of \textit{Opus-SMDP} and \textit{GPT-SMDP} are equivalent, we replicated the original data ($n=162$, 15 raters) by factors
$k=1,2,5,10,13,14,15,20,25,30,35,36,37,38,39,40$.  
For each $k$, the entire rater block was duplicated $k$ times, each copy receiving a new \texttt{evaluator\_code}.  
Fresh random intercepts $u_{0j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{evaluator}})$ were drawn independently for every new rater, yielding the simulated response

\[
y_{ij}=X_{ij}\beta+u_{0j}+\varepsilon_{ij},\qquad
\varepsilon_{ij}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon}\bigr).
\]

Within a Monte-Carlo loop (1\,000 replicates per $k$) we conducted two one-sided tests (TOST) with an equivalence margin of $\Delta=\pm0.13$ (20 \% of the residual SD, $\sigma\approx0.67$).  
\emph{Equivalence power} was the proportion of replicates whose 90 \% confidence interval lay entirely inside $\pm\Delta$.

