\paragraph*{Summary of A5.1 Results}

Pairwise effects Opus-SMDP vs.\ GPT-zero ($d\approx0.90$) and GPT-zero vs.\ GPT-SMDP ($d\approx-0.84$) achieved 100 \% power (95 \% CI: 99.63–100 \%).  
In contrast, Opus-SMDP vs.\ GPT-SMDP ($d\approx0.06$) showed only 3.9 \% power (95 \% CI: 2.8–5.3 \%).  
For the equivalence test (OVR), power reached 0.85 at $k=25$ ($\approx375$ raters) and 0.95 at $k\ge38$ ($\ge570$ raters).

\bigskip
%--------------------------------------------------
% A5.2 Counseling-Evaluation AI Scores
%--------------------------------------------------
\subsection*{A5.2 Counseling-Evaluation AI Scores}

Using the counseling-evaluation AI ratings, we evaluated power for detecting the difference between human and large-language-model (LLM) evaluators on the OVR score.
The mixed model employed was

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cai_model}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Score}_{ij}
  &= \beta_{0}
   + \beta_{1}\,\mathrm{model}_{\text{star},ij}
   + \beta_{2}\,\mathrm{model}_{\text{sun},ij} \\
  &\quad + \beta_{3}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{40s,ij}
   + \beta_{4}\,\mathrm{AgeCat}_{60s,ij}
   + \beta_{5}\,\mathrm{Sex}_{\text{male},ij} \\
  &\quad + u_{0j}
   + \varepsilon_{ij},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

where $u_{0j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{evaluator}})$ and $\varepsilon_{ij}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{\varepsilon})$.  
Separate random intercepts \\ \texttt{evaluator\_human} and \texttt{evaluator\_llm} allowed for differing rater variances.  
With the observed effect size, Monte-Carlo simulations (1\,000 replicates) indicated power close to 100 \% for detecting the human-versus-LLM main effect under the current sample size ($N=162$).

\paragraph*{Summary of A5.2 Results}

Given the estimated effect magnitude and variance structure, the human–versus–LLM contrast on the OVR score attained essentially 100\,\% power in the original design, except when GPT-SMDP was rated by o3, where the power was 79\,\%; further increases in sample size yielded negligible gains.



