\begin{abstract}
  The \emph{medial axis} of a smoothly embedded surface in $\Rspace^3$ consists of all points for which the Euclidean distance function on the surface has at least two minima.
  We generalize this notion to the \emph{mid-sphere axis}, which consists of all points for which the Euclidean distance function has two interchanging saddles that swap their partners in the pairing by persistent homology.
  It offers a discrete-algebraic multi-scale approach to computing ridge-like structures on the surface.
  As a proof of concept, an algorithm that computes stair-case approximations of the mid-sphere axis is provided.

  \keywords{Medial axes, Morse functions, persistent homology, vineyards, computation.}

\end{abstract}


