\section{Introduction}


%% intro about LLMs and their potential
The impressive performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) in tasks like question-answering ~\cite{choi2021chatgpt,blair2023can} and mathematical reasoning ~\cite{ahn-etal-2024-large,zhu-etal-2023-solving} has driven their widespread adoption across various domains ~\cite{durante2024agent}. Previously, these tasks required specialized models and large, labeled datasets. LLMs, with their in-context learning, now perform diverse tasks without additional training, using only task instructions and examples in prompts ~\cite{zhao2023survey}. As a result, LLMs are now integral to real-world applications, such as chatbots simulating human conversation ~\cite{li2024efficient}.

% mention harmfulness of LLMs
As LLMs reshape information processing ~\cite{naveed2023comprehensive}, challenges in their end-user applications have emerged, including amplified biases, misuse in spreading misinformation, and ethical concerns over harmful content generation ~\cite{10.1145/3442188.3445922,yao2024survey,gallegos-etal-2024-bias}. To address these challenges, advancements have focused on aligning LLM outputs with ethical standards and minimizing harmful content. For example, instructed LLMs may refuse to respond if a reply could be harmful or damaging, instead issuing statements like, ``\textit{I am sorry, but as an AI language model, I cannot use hurtful or disrespectful language}'' ~\cite{deshpande-etal-2023-toxicity,10.1145/3613904.3642135}. In our study, we define this kind of response as \textit{refusal behavior}. Instruction-tuned LLMs may still generate toxic responses in some cases, despite refusing to do so in others ~\cite{wei2023jailbroken}. Studies have shown that specific prompt designs can bypass LLM safeguards ~\cite{rai2024guardian,lin2025against,10759678,wei2023jailbroken}, leading to the generation of inappropriate content ~\cite{derner2024security}. Similarly, previous research has found that incorporating a persona into the prompt can significantly increase the toxicity of ChatGPT's responses ~\cite{deshpande-etal-2023-toxicity}. For example, adopting the persona of boxer Muhammad Ali in a prompt can increase toxicity by up to six times, leading to more inappropriate responses. This is particularly concerning given the accessibility of LLMs via APIs, which allow users to define system roles and customize the model's persona, thus amplifying these risks ~\cite{deshpande-etal-2023-toxicity,10.1145/3613904.3642036}.

%  mention scarcity of studies focusing on non-Western centric models
Most studies have focused on Western-based LLMs and societies, with limited research on Chinese-based models and their interaction with Chinese society ~\cite{zhou2024political,tan2024chinesesafetyqasafetyshortform,pawar2024survey}. Given China's diverse population, it is crucial to conduct culturally specific evaluations of Chinese-based LLMs. Additionally, few studies have explored how persona assignment impacts safeguard mechanisms--such as refusal behavior and toxicity--in these models. 

% goal of this paper, research questions and contributions
To investigate these aspects, this study focuses on the Qwen model, one of the most popular LLMs in China, developed by Alibaba\footnote{\url{https://www.autonomous.ai/ourblog/explore-qwen-model-china-biggest-ai-model}}. Specifically, we examine \textbf{Qwen-Turbo}\footnote{\url{https://help.aliyun.com/zh/dashscope/developer-reference/api-details?spm=a2c4g.11186623.0.0.b22a3116jp5wvR}}, the commercially optimized variant of the Qwen family, which as of 2024 is deployed by over 90,000 enterprises and actively used in domains like enterprise chatbots, content generation, and personal assistants\footnote{\url{https://www.alibabagroup.com/en-US/document-1725671468706037760}}.
Recently, Alibaba announced a collaboration with Apple to integrate its Qwen AI models into the iPhone lineup in mainland China\footnote{\url{https://www.reuters.com/technology/artificial-intelligence/alibaba-chairman-confirms-ai-partnership-with-apple-chinese-iphones-2025-02-13/}}, potentially reshaping AI feature implementation in one of the world’s most regulated tech markets. Given Qwen's significant deployment across China and its growing relevance in global downstream applications ~\cite{liu2025comparing,nakanishi2025analyzing}, it is increasingly important for both Chinese and international researchers to evaluate its safety mechanisms. 

In this study, we systematically investigate how different elements of prompt design---\textit{prompt templates} (structured textual instructions), \textit{designated personas} (assigned identities or roles), and \textit{target social groups} (specific to the Chinese social context)---influence refusal behavior and toxicity in Qwen. Focusing on Qwen-Turbo as a representative and widely adopted model, this study proposes a systematic, quantitative approach to address the following research questions:

\begin{itemize}
     \item \textbf{RQ1:} How do prompt \textit{templates} and designated \textit{personas} affect the refusal rate in Qwen?
     \item \textbf{RQ2:} How do prompt \textit{templates} and designated \textit{personas} affect the level of toxicity in Qwen?
    \item \textbf{RQ3:} How different elements of prompt design --- prompt \textit{templates}, designated \textit{personas} and target \textit{social groups} --- drive the refusal behavior and toxicity in Qwen?
    \item \textbf{RQ4:} How can the safety of Qwen be improved by leveraging auxiliary LLMs?    
\end{itemize}

% may need to rewrite this part. 
Our contributions are as follows: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Empirical investigation of refusal behaviors:} We fine-tuned a BERT-based classifier to detect refusal patterns in Qwen's responses, uncovering gender biases across different persona categories. 

    \item \textbf{Quantitative analysis of persona-driven toxicity:} We examined how persona assignment affects Qwen's toxicity, finding significant increases — up to 60 times — for certain \textit{persona + social group} combinations. 

    \item \textbf{Identification of key determinants of refusal and toxicity:} Through regression analyses, we identified key factors influencing Qwen's refusal and toxicity behaviors, focusing on persona categories, social groups, and prompt templates. 

    \item \textbf{Impact of a multi-model mitigation framework:} We assessed a multi-model strategy involving iterative feedback between Qwen and an external evaluator, effectively reducing toxic outputs. 
\end{itemize}


% outline
The outline of this paper is as follows: in Section~\ref{sec:related}, we review related literature. We then describe the data collection methods and the analysis and mitigation methods in Section~\ref{sec:methods}. A detailed presentation of the experimental results is then provided in Section~\ref{sec:results}. We then discuss the contributions and implications of our work, outline its limitations, propose future research directions, and report ethical implications in Sections~\ref{sec:discussion} and \ref{sec:ethical considerations}. 

