\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

% \subsection{Persona assigment}  
\subsection{Assigning personas to LLMs}  

Persona-Assigned LLMs are large language models conditioned or adapted to generate responses from the perspective of a specific persona by incorporating persona-specific instructions in the prompt (e.g., ``\textit{Take the role of an elementary-school tutor}'') ~\cite{gupta2024personabias}.  These ``persona-assigned LLMs'' not only enhance interactions through personalization but also have a wide array of practical applications due to their potential to mimic human behavior ~\cite{gupta2024personabias}. 



~\cite{tseng2024two} divided studies on personas in the LLM era into two categories: the first is \textit{LLM Role-Playing}, where LLMs are assigned specific roles (e.g., judge or nurse) and are required to act according to environmental feedback, adapting to the context; the second is \textit{LLM Personalization}, where LLMs adopt user-specific personas (e.g., background information or historical behaviors) to tailor responses and meet individualized needs, adapting to different users. Through role-playing, large language models (LLMs) can generate responses aligned with assigned roles, adapt to diverse interactive environments, and collaborate in multi-agent scenarios ~\cite{park2023generative,fontana2024nicer,guo2024large}. Regarding personalized LLM, previous studies have explored their use in various applications, such as recommendation systems, search, education, healthcare, and dialogue ~\cite{wang2023zero,ziems-etal-2023-large,abbasian2023conversational,hudecek-dusek-2023-large}. These studies emphasize tailored responses and user-specific adaptations achieved through role-based personalization and prompt tuning, which not only help create convincing in-game characters ~\cite{10.1145/3402942.3409599} but also enable simulated environments for assessing the reception of novel products ~\cite{10.1145/3526113.3545616} and evaluating scientific hypotheses~\cite{10.1145/3670865.3673513}. 

Our research aligns more closely with the second case. %taxonomy categorizing personas in research contexts. 
We adopted personas from ~\cite{deshpande-etal-2023-toxicity}, representing historical or contemporary figures such as Muhammad Ali and Adolf Hitler. These personas were translated into Chinese, integrating them as system roles within the Qwen framework. We then collected responses from Qwen to template-based prompts such as \textit{``Say something about \{S\}''}, after assigning personas. The responses were subsequently analyzed, based on the assigned personas, in terms of: (1) the refusal rate and (2) the toxicity of the response content.



\subsection{Detecting harmful behavior in LLMs}


%% toxicity detection for English -> Toxicity detection for Chinese -> Our Choice. 


Recent research has demonstrated that LLMs raise ethical concerns and may have potentially harmful impacts on society ~\cite{luong-etal-2024-realistic,10.1145/3691620.3695018}. In response, several tools have been developed to detect and quantify toxic language, such as the Perspective API\footnote{\url{https://perspectiveapi.com/}} and OpenAI’s Moderation API\footnote{\url{https://github.com/openai/moderation-api-release}}. For example, a recent study by ~\cite{deshpande-etal-2023-toxicity} conducted the first large-scale analysis of ChatGPT, relying on the Perspective API to examine its toxicity after assigning different personas. Their findings showed that persona assignment could lead to significantly higher toxicity, with toxicity levels increasing by as much as six times compared to the default settings. 

Regarding Chinese-language toxicity detection, ~\cite{deng-etal-2022-cold} introduced a dataset and developed a fine-tuned model, COLDETECTOR, which classifies content into two categories: 0 (Non-Offensive) and 1 (Offensive). While COLDETECTOR is specifically designed for Chinese-language content and performs well in binary classification tasks, it lacks the granularity needed to analyze subtle variations across different prompt elements such as social group, persona, and template.

To address this limitation, we adopt the Perspective API in this study. Although originally developed for English, the API supports multilingual toxicity detection and provides continuous toxicity scores that allow for more nuanced comparisons. Prior work has demonstrated its applicability to Chinese-language content in both policy impact analysis and content moderation tools ~\cite{yu2024topic,li2024demod}.


\subsection{Mitigating harmfulness of LLMs}

Earlier efforts have included fine-tuning pre-trained models ~\cite{gehman-etal-2020-realtoxicityprompts, gururangan-etal-2020-dont}, steering model generation toward less toxic outputs, or using post-hoc methods such as filtering toxic content at runtime ~\cite{pascual-etal-2021-plug-play, krause-etal-2021-gedi-generative}. Additionally, reinforcement learning with human feedback has been applied to reduce toxic language generation ~\cite{faal2023reward}. All of the above-mentioned methods rely on accessing the specific information in LLMs, which could be not available for a given model, such as the one chosen in our study. The above mentioned methods have limitations, including but not limited to high computational costs, instability, and potential disruption of text coherence. Given those constraints, we adopt an \textit{AI-agent-based} approach ~\cite{liu2023agentbench}, which dynamically refines responses through iterative feedback mechanisms. The most similar approach to ours is presented in ~\cite{owens2024multi, borah-mihalcea-2024-towards}, where the authors explore methods to mitigate stereotypes through the interaction of multiple large language models (LLMs). In ~\cite{owens2024multi}, a multi-LLM debiasing framework is introduced, which includes both centralized and decentralized strategies. The main distinction between these two approaches lies in the established communication pattern among LLMs, typically implemented a model evaluating another: the centralized model involves communication exclusively with a central model, while the decentralized approach allows for direct interaction among all models.  For example, in the centralized approach, model B and model C generate responses independently but only communicate with a central model A. In contrast, in the decentralized approach, models A, B, and C interact directly with each other, exchanging feedback iteratively to refine their responses collaboratively.  Inspired by their work, we adopt and extend a centralized approach to address toxic outputs from Qwen's, using the toxicity values measured by the Perspective API. 



