%Multi-modal satellite imagery provides complementary perspectives of Earth's surface through diverse sensing modalities, offering unprecedented opportunities for land cover classification and environmental monitoring. Yet two critical challenges hinder progress: the scarcity of labeled training data and the complex relationships between different sensor modalities and their multi-label annotations. In this paper, we propose a unified contrastive learning framework that addresses both challenges by integrating unsupervised intra-modality objectives with supervised inter-modality contrastive loss. Our approach explicitly preserves modality-specific features—such as structural patterns in SAR imagery and spectral signatures in optical data—while establishing semantic connections across modalities through partial label correlations. We evaluate our method on the refined BigEarthNet (reBEN) and SENT12MS dataset, demonstrating strong performance under severe data scarcity. Even with only 10\% of the labeled data, our model achieves comparable or superior results to fully supervised baselines. Our results highlight the effectiveness of joint modality-label contrastive learning in improving representation quality and reducing dependence on large-scale annotations in remote sensing applications.

% \noindent We propose a novel contrastive learning framework for advanced Earth observation (EO) using multi-modal satellite imagery. Our framework addresses two key challenges in EO: the scarcity of labeled training data and the difficulty of aligning semantically similar images across sensing modalities with distinct characteristics. Specifically, we integrate unsupervised contrastive learning within each modality and supervised contrastive learning across modalities using multi-label annotations. Our method explicitly preserves modality-specific features—such as structural patterns in SAR imagery and spectral signatures in optical data—while enforcing \emph{semantic alignment}, i.e., learning shared representations for the same land cover classes (e.g., forest, urban) even when captured through different sensors. This alignment bridges the modality gap and enables the model to generalize better in downstream tasks. We evaluate our framework on the refined BigEarthNet (reBEN) and SENT12MS datasets, demonstrating strong performance under severe data scarcity. With only 10\% of the labeled data, our model achieves comparable or superior results to fully supervised baselines. These results highlight the effectiveness of joint modality-label contrastive learning in improving representation quality and reducing dependence on large-scale annotations in remote sensing applications. 


\begin{abstract}
Contrastive learning (CL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for learning transferable representations without the reliance on large labeled datasets. Its ability to capture intrinsic similarities and differences among data samples has led to state-of-the-art results in computer vision tasks. These strengths make CL particularly well-suited for Earth System Observation (ESO), where diverse satellite modalities such as optical and SAR imagery offer naturally aligned views of the same geospatial regions. However, ESO presents unique challenges, including high inter-class similarity, scene clutter, and ambiguous boundaries, which complicate representation learning—especially in low-label, multi-label settings. Existing CL frameworks often focus on intra-modality self-supervision or lack mechanisms for multi-label alignment and semantic precision across modalities. In this work, we introduce MoSAiC, a unified framework that jointly optimizes intra- and inter-modality contrastive learning with a multi-label supervised contrastive loss. Designed specifically for multi-modal satellite imagery, MoSAiC enables finer semantic disentanglement and more robust representation learning across spectrally similar and spatially complex classes. Experiments on two benchmark datasets, BigEarthNet V2.0 and Sent12MS, show that MoSAiC consistently outperforms both fully supervised and self-supervised baselines in terms of accuracy, cluster coherence, and generalization in low-label and high-class-overlap scenarios.
\end{abstract}


