at two or more land cover categories reflect and absorb light in near-identical ways, making them hard to distinguish. For particularly BigEarthNet dataset, such spectral similar classes are visually ambiguous leading difficulty for manual annotation, and increase the uncertainty in prediction \cite{probabilistic}.Recent studies on BigEarthNet demonstrate that high spectral similarity among land cover classes significantly reduces deep learning model performance, leading to frequent confusion and misclassification~\cite{sumbul2021bigearthnet,dimitrovski2023land,papoutsis2022benchmarking,chaudhuri2021multilabel}. Classes such as 'Permanent crops','Complex cultivation patterns', and 'Agro-forestry areas" exhibited notably poor performance due to overlapping spectral signatures, causing substantial misclassification even with advanced CNN architectures~\cite{papoutsis2022benchmarking,chaudhuri2021multilabel,dimitrovski2023land}. In such cases, a model might correctly detect vegetation but struggle to assign the right subclass label. Even human analysts find it difficult to distinguish certain land/water categories when spectra overlap (e.g. turbid water vs. soil)\cite{probabilistic}.Another clear example is that the class “Agro-forestry and natural vegetation” (mixed agriculture/natural vegetation) often confused with “Complex cultivation patterns”, likely because both can contain cultivated crops at certain times\cite{chaudhuri2021interband} for instance during the summer season captured by Sentinel-2, a patch of semi-natural vegetation with some fields can look spectrally akin to a patch of mosaic farmland – the CNN had trouble separating them based on spectral data alone. Similarly, 'broad-leaved forest' was often confused with 'mixed forest', because mixed forests contain a subset of broadleaf trees making their spectral signals overlap\cite{chaudhuri2021interband}. In this work we have shown the numerical evidence of these claims by introducing class similarity matrix on the 40\% training samples that we trained our model on Figure~\ref{fig:class_similarity}. The matrix has been calculated using euclidean distance applied on mean of the spectral bands for each pixel annotated images. It can be clearly seen that 'Broad-leaved-forest (BF)','Mixed Forest (MF)', and 'Coniferous Forest (CF)' are highly similar according to their nature and spectral bands, which raises the uncertainity to assign a sub-class type even the model recognize the patch as forest. The projection of these distance and similarity matrix for SENT12MS dataset can be found in Appendix~\ref{apendix:sim_SENT12MS}. For further analysis, we employed the Hamming Distance and Brier Score to assess label-wise accuracy and probabilistic calibration across 19 land cover classes. The Hamming Distance measures the proportion of incorrect label predictions, providing a direct indicator of classification errors, while the Brier Score quantifies the mean squared difference between predicted probabilities and true labels, emphasizing the model's confidence and calibration quality. These metrics are crucial for understanding class-specific performance and identifying areas for improvement in complex datasets like bigEarthNet, where spectral similarities can lead to misclassifications. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:results}, the model excels for distinct classes, such as Coastal Wetlands (CW) and Beaches, Dunes, Sands (BDS), achieving near-perfect scores with a Hamming Distance of 0.00\% and 0.20\%, and Brier Scores of 0.01\% and 0.02\%, respectively. However, it struggles with heterogeneous classes like Transitional Woodland, Shrub (TWS) and Land Principally Occupied by Agriculture, with Significant Areas of Natural Vegetation (LPOB), exhibiting high error rates (Hamming Distance of 33.50\% and 30.60\%, Brier Scores of 20.63\% and 17.33\%, respectively). Classes such as Mixed Forest (MF) and Coniferous Forest (CF) also show elevated errors (Hamming Distance of 30.60\% and 28.80\%), reflecting challenges due to spectral overlap. 
