\section{Introduction}
In recent years, artificial intelligence has demonstrated formidable capabilities across various tasks, achieving performance levels comparable to or even surpassing top human expertise - whether in recognition/generation tasks for speech~\citep{chu2023qwen} and images~\citep{Foret2020SharpnessAwareMF}, or knowledge-intensive tasks such as translation~\citep{Silver2017MasteringTG}, Go strategy~\citep{Takase2021LessonsOP}, and mathematical olympiad problem solving~\citep{Trinh2024SolvingOG}. 
The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has particularly propelled AI capabilities to unprecedented heights, catalyzing a new wave of artificial general intelligence (AGI) development.

The training paradigm for LLMs typically comprises three critical phases: 
pretraining, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning. 
Pretraining primarily enhances the model's knowledge, SFT empowers superior instruction-following capabilities, while reinforcement learning aligns model responses with human values through preference optimization.
Although LLMs exhibit remarkable knowledge capacity through pretraining on massive unstructured data, the inherent limitations of unstructured text - including low information density and ambiguous knowledge representation - hinder precise acquisition of domain-specific knowledge. 
Structured databases offer a promising solution to this challenge. 
Taking PubChem~\citep{kim2019pubchem}, a chemical database as an example, it contains over 100 million molecular entities, each characterized by standardized fields (e.g., SMILES~\citep{weininger1988smiles}, physicochemical properties, reaction pathways) that form an intricate knowledge network. 
This structured representation inherently achieves significantly higher information density compared to unstructured text.

Structured databases often encapsulate meticulously curated knowledge and experimental data of immense potential value - painstakingly accumulated by generations of top scientists. 
These resources contain substantial under-explored information. 
However, existing methodologies fail to fully exploit this wealth of data. 
Representative works like MoLFormer~\citep{ross2022large} primarily utilize molecular representations for pretraining while neglecting more critical knowledge dimensions such as molecular properties.
Meanwhile, reinforcement learning, though less data-hungry than pretraining and SFT, remains computationally intensive due to the substantial human annotation required for its preference data.

To address these dual challenges, we propose RLDBF (Reinforcement Learning with Database Feedback), a novel framework that synergizes structured databases with reinforcement learning. 
Our principal contributions are threefold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We pioneer the concept of leveraging database data to alleviate LLMs' data bottleneck issues, providing new perspectives for exploiting knowledge in structured databases. 
    This may inspire future exploration of database-driven knowledge enhancement.
    \item We propose the first reinforcement learning framework integrated with structured databases for LLM enhancement. 
    Our meticulously designed architecture enables efficient knowledge absorption from databases while substantially reducing annotation overhead.
    \item Multi-dimensional experiments demonstrate our method's effectiveness. 
    Notably, models trained with RLDBF not only exhibit superior generalization on chemical tasks but also reveal emergent capabilities for assisting in-depth chemical reasoning.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}

\subsection{AI for Chemistry}

The chemistry and materials science communities have witnessed the growing adoption of Transformer-based language models for various downstream tasks, including crystal structure prediction and coulombic efficiency prediction. These approaches can be broadly categorized by their sequence processing strategies: 1) post-training on foundation large language models (LLMs) with data encoded via pre-trained tokenizer, or 2) developing task-specific tokenizer and encoding schemes coupled with custom-defined models.

As demonstrated by \citet{dagan2024getting}, the distributional mismatch between natural language-trained tokenizer and domain-specific tasks can significantly degrade downstream performance. This has driven researchers to design specialized sub-word vocabularies and train compact Transformer-based architectures for specific applications. For instance, \citet{antunes2024crystal} developed a 25M-parameter GPT-like model trained on crystallographic information for structure generation. \citet{liu2025alchembert} achieved superior materials property prediction through a 110M-parameter BERT model trained on crystal structures and natural language descriptions. Three-dimensional generative capabilities were demonstrated by \citet{flam2023language}, while \citet{flam2023atom} pioneered protein-drug conjugates generation using specialized architectures.

However, the prohibitive computational resource requirements for large-scale pre-training have constrained model development. Post-training paradigms offer more accessible alternatives, benefiting from scaling laws where larger models capture more information. \citet{gruver2024fine} leverages post-trained LLMs to comprehend and validate three-dimensional atomic configurations, demonstrating the scaling law governing model capacity and structural fidelity in preserving critical crystal symmetries.

Recent efforts have focused on domain adaptation through curated datasets (e.g., textbooks, research papers, and QA pairs) to create chemistry-specific LLMs. \citet{zhang2024chemllm} fine-tuned \citet{cai2024internlm2} with 7M proprietary chemical instructions for chemical QA tasks. \citet{zhao2024chemdfm} conducted incremental pretraining and instruction tuning on Llama2 13B using extensive chemical corpora. Specialized applications include GPT-3 fine-tuning for electrochemical property prediction by \citet{xie2023large}, and text-encoded atomistic data adaptation of Llama2 7B-70B for stable crystal generation by \citet{gruver2024fine}, demonstrating that model scales positively correlated with symmetry preservation capabilities.

\subsection{Structured Data Integration}

Integrating structured/sub-structured data into natural language pre-trained LLMs presents unique challenges. Conventional approaches often naively serialize structured data into text sequences, as exemplified by \citet{ren2024watergpt}. However, \citet{mirchandani2023large} revealed through the ARC benchmark\citep{chollet2019measure} that LLMs exhibit varying sensitivity to structural semantics, complicating direct alignment between structured data representations and textual embeddings.

To bridge this semantic gap, \citet{sun2021ernie} incorporated knowledge graph triples via autoencoder networks, though scalability remains constrained by structured data requirements. Alternative strategies leverage LLMs to generate synthetic text from structured input, as demonstrated in the synthesis of clinical data by \citet{kweon2023publicly}. Recent advances address hallucination in synthetic data through structured data verification, as proposed by \citet{chenknowledge}, enabling higher-fidelity QA pair generation.

\subsection{Numerical Domain Knowledge}

The integration of experimentally derived or computationally generated structured data, often requiring precision to multiple decimal places, exposes critical limitations in LLM numerical reasoning. \citet{zheng2025large} systematically identified deficiencies in chemical numerical tasks. This highlights the fundamental challenge of achieving high-precision numerical memorization and reasoning in AI4Science applications.

Emerging research quantifies these limitations: \citet{yuan2023well} documented systematic underperformance in numerical operations, while \citet{sakamoto2025development} revealed deficiencies in numerical common sense reasoning. Domain-specific challenges are addressed by \citet{mishra2020towards}, identifying chemically grounded numerical tasks (e.g., stoichiometric balancing, molecular weight calculation) where current models struggle without explicit integration of chemical knowledge.

