\subsection{Q\&A Template Construction}

The data in databases is highly structured, while LLMs are trained on unstructured text. 
A natural approach is to transform structured data into unstructured training data via template filling. 
We used GPT-4o to generate multiple Q\&A templates. 
To boost model performance on diverse instructions, GPT-4o was instructed to create templates beneficial for subsequent fine - tuning, ensuring diversity in style, structure, and expression. 
Next, we applied a three - step filter: 
1) For templates with identical questions, only one was kept; 
2) Templates with general yes - no questions were removed to prevent the model from only learning yes/no answers; 
3)Templates were manually checked, and those with unreasonable content or wording errors were deleted.
Finally, 219 templates were generated, examples are shown in appendix Table~\ref{gpt_templates}.
To increase the difficulty of subsequent tests, we split these templates into training and test sets. 
Specifically, we first used 
sentence-transformers\footnote{https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2}~\citep{reimers-2019-sentence-bert} 
to embed each question template, then clustered them using the DBSCAN algorithm. 
We adjusted the algorithm's parameters to get a reasonable number of outliers, using the corresponding templates to construct the test set. 
Ultimately, we obtained 184 training templates and 35 test templates.

\subsection{CPT \& SFT Data Synthesis}

Continual Pre-Training (CPT) and SFT are two common methods for training LLMs. 
To explore the impact of different training data synthesis methods and establish a comprehensive benchmark for subsequent tests, we designed various ways to synthesize training data based on the templates from the previous subsection.
\paragraph{CPT Data}
The main purpose of CPT is to equip LLMs with more knowledge. 
We tried five methods to help LLMs better establish connections among compounds, properties, and values:
1) Directly concatenating Q\&A pairs;
2) Directly concatenating molecule, property, and value;
3) \& 4) Using question templates with numerical answers only. 
To help LLMs better connect questions with real answers and avoid them only outputting answers, we added "Answer the question only with the corresponding value" after each question.
This was also applied in synthesizing SFT and RL data. 
We then decorated Q\&A pairs with "User-Assistant" or "**";
5) Directly building data with "The [property] of [compound] is [value]" template.
\paragraph{SFT Data} 
We tried three methods:
1) Directly using Q\&A pairs;
2) Using Q\&A pairs with answer templates added to questions as prompts to reduce the impact on the model's instruction - following ability;
3) Using question templates with only numerical answers.
The synthesis methods above and examples are summarized in appendix Figure~\ref{cptsft_syn}.

\subsection{RLDBF}

\begin{figure}[t]
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{rldbf_dpoframe.pdf}
\end{center}
\caption{Overview of RLDBF.}
\label{dpo_framework}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Motivation}
The feedback in Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is one-way, only indicating correct answers, not wrong ones. 
Though effective, it has limitations. 
Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)~\citep{ouyang2022training} enables models to reject incorrect outputs, enhancing the safety and stability of their outputs. 
It allows models to learn both correct answers and how to avoid wrong ones.
Inspired by this, we decided to employ reinforcement learning to better leverage database data for model training. 
However, RLHF relies heavily on high quality human preference labels, which increases data acquisition costs and exposes model performance to annotator bias due to the lack of factual basis in data comparison. 
Considering this, we proposed Reinforcement Learning from DataBase Feedback (RLDBF), using databases to automatically build preference data.
Databases are natural data labelers. 
We can create many preference data pairs from different database instances and establish their priority by disturbing attributes and values.
Take these two data entries as an example: 
"[Ins. A]-[Attr. 1]-[Attr. 2]; [Ins. B]-[Attr. 1]-[Attr. 2]". 
Based on the severity of knowledge-based errors, the preference data can be constructed as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Score 3 (result match): [Attr. 1] of [Ins. A] is [Attr. 1 Value of A]
    \item Score 2 (instance match, attribute mismatch): [Attr. 1] of [Ins A] is Value(Attr. 2, A)
    \item Score 1 (instance mismatch, attribute match): [Attr. 1] of [Ins A] is Value(Attr. 1, B)
    \item Score 0 (all mismatch): [Attr. 1] of [Ins A] is Value(Attr. 2, B)
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Implementation}
RLAIF~\citep{lee2023rlaif} proposes to directly use the feedback from a LLM as the reward signal in RL.
This approach not only reduces the overhead associated with AI preference labeling and reward model training (which is essentially a form of distillation) but also demonstrates superior performance compared to learning reward models through distillation.
This finding aligns with our perspective, as training a reward model on database data may introduce additional biases.
To address this, we adopt a more streamlined approach through Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)~\citep{rafailov2023direct}, enabling direct discrimination between correct and erroneous data without intermediary models.
We enhance output fidelity by exclusively constructing preference pairs using verified numerical ground truths—even for "reject" samples—thereby minimizing noise from stochastic perturbations. 
Empirical analysis reveals that LLMs exhibit limited discriminative capacity for molecular structures due to multi-token compositional representations, frequently conflating properties of structurally analogous molecules. 
To mitigate this, we strategically generate preference pairs by contrasting target molecules' verified property values with those of their nearest structural analogs.
Specifically, for each specific molecule $C$, we first identify $K$ most similar molecule $\{S_i\}$.
Then, for a given property $P$ of molecule $C$, we construct $K$ preference pairs, where the "chosen" value in each pair is the ground truth value, and the "reject" value is the value of property $P$ from $\{S_i\}$. 
Finally, to minimize the influence of textual descriptors, the preference pairs contain only the specific numerical values without additional words.
These constructed pairs finally serve as the DPO data for model learning. 
Through the above steps, we have implemented the entire process of RLDBF.

\paragraph{Comparable Alternatives}
We also proposed several alternative strategies for constructing "reject" data, including:
1) Using values of different properties for the same molecule, values of the same property for different molecules, and values of different properties for different molecules (SMILES remains unchanged);
2) Using the same molecule with different properties, different molecules with the same property, and different molecules with different properties;
3) Only using the same molecule with different properties and different molecules with the same property;
4) Only using the same molecule with different properties and the most similar different molecule with the same property;
5) Adding answer templates to the instruction part based on 4);
6) Refining 5) by only using the most similar different molecule with the same property.
Examples are shown in appendix Figure~\ref{dpo_syn}.
These construction methods are also used as benchmarks for subsequent tests.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Dataset}

\subsubsection{Pubchem}

PubChem~\citep{kim2019pubchem} is an open-access chemical database, systematically integrates structural, physicochemical, and bioactivity data for small molecules from 750+ authoritative sources. 
Its triad architecture (Compound, Substance, BioAssay) enables cross-domain exploration of structure-biological activity relationships through curated experimental results and predictive models. 
PubChem has become fundamental infrastructure for computational chemistry and translational studies, ensuring reproducibility while bridging experimental and computational research paradigms.

In this study, we constructed datasets using downloadable PubChem data~\footnote{https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/docs/downloads} 
due to its substantial volume and accessibility.
The processed data was partitioned into multiple files, each containing 500,000 molecules. 
We first examined the key-value pairs of each entry, selecting non-empty keys to ensure data integrity for subsequent experiments. 
Five molecular properties were ultimately chosen for dataset construction: 
Hydrogen Bond Acceptor Count, Hydrogen Bond Donor Count, Rotatable Bond Count, Octanol-water Partition Coefficient, and Molecular Weight. 
These properties can also be computationally derived. 
Additionally, SMILES~\citep{weininger1988smiles} was adopted as the molecular textual representation.

\paragraph{Training Set}
Under computational resource constraints, we maximized the training dataset size within feasible limits, ultimately constructing it based on the first 10,000 molecules from PubChem.

\paragraph{Test Set}
The test set was systematically designed along two dimensions with four difficulty levels. 
The two dimensions distinguish between in-domain molecules (from the training set) and out-domain molecules (from PubChem molecules beyond the 500,000th entry), enabling evaluation of both model learning capacity and generalization ability.
Each question requires the model to predict specific molecular properties, with four progressively challenging levels:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Level 1: Distractors involve basic numerical perturbations of the correct answer: $\pm0.1, \times10, \div10$
    \item Level 2: Sophisticated perturbations include nine variants: $\pm0.1, \pm1, \times(-1)$, and combined operations ($\times(-1)$ followed by $\pm0.1 / \pm1$)
    \item Level 3: Distractors comprise values from different properties of the same molecule, same properties of different molecules, or different properties of different molecules;
    \item Level 4: Distractors are selected from the most similar molecules' corresponding properties
\end{itemize}
To mitigate random bias, we implemented three independent sampling repetitions for each dimension-level combination, generating 200 questions per sampling instance. 
We also calculate the weighted sum for comparison:
\begin{equation}
    W.S. = 0.4*S_4 + 0.3*S_3 + 0.2*S_2 + 0.1*S_1,
\end{equation}
where $S_i$ is the sum of the average scores of Level i.

\subsubsection{ChemBench}

ChemBench~\citep{zhang2024chemllm} is a comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate the performance of AI models in chemistry-related tasks. 
It encompasses a diverse set of problems, including 
Name Conversion(NC), Property Prediction(PP1), Mol2caption(M2C), Caption2mol(C2M), Product Prediction(PP2), Retrosynthesis(RS), Yield Prediction(YP), Temperature Prediction(TP) and Solvent Prediction(SP).
This benchmark provides a rigorous assessment of model capabilities in the chemical domain, and facilitates standardized comparisons across different approaches, promoting advancements in AI-driven chemistry research.
Results are rounded to the nearest tenth.

\subsection{Training Setup}
Our experiments were conducted on a computing cluster equipped with 8×NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs, utilizing the Llama3-8B-Instruct~\citep{grattafiori2024llama} model as the base model. 
To optimize experimental efficiency, we initially performed preliminary investigations to determine appropriate learning rate ranges. 
The final learning rates for both the CPT and RL were selected from [1e-7, 5e-7, 1e-6], while SFT employed learning rates from [1e-7, 1e-6]. 
For each candidate learning rate configuration, we trained corresponding model instances for 1 epoch and subsequently selected the optimal performing model for comparative analysis. 
Throughout the training process, all model parameters remained activated and trainable, the DeepSpeed ZeRO-1\citep{rasley2020deepspeed} are employed.

\subsection{Baselines}
In addition to the trained models discussed in previous sections, we conducted comprehensive comparative analyses with other prominent LLMs within our benchmarking framework. 
These models include: 
GPT-4o-mini~\citep{hurst2024gpt},
GPT-3.5-Turbo,
Qwen2-7B-Instruct~\citep{yang2024qwen2technicalreport},
Llama3-8B-Instruct; 
and domain-specific chemical models comprising
KALE-LM-Chem~\citep{dai2024kale}, 
ChemDFM~\citep{zhao2024chemdfm},
ChemLLM~\citep{zhang2024chemllm},
and LlaSMol-Mistral-7B~\citep{yu2024llasmol}.

