\paragraph{PubChem Results}
As shown in Table~\ref{exp_mc}, general models like GPT-4o-mini and GPT-3.5-Turbo exhibit performance degradation as task complexity increases, particularly in Level 3 and Level 4 scenarios. 
For instance, GPT-4o-mini achieves 67.8\% accuracy in Level 1 (In-domain) but drops to 27.0\% in Level 4 (In-domain), indicating limited robustness against sophisticated perturbations. 
Domain-specific models such as KALE-LM-Chem and ChemDFM show moderate improvements in Level 4 (Out-domain) compared to GPT, yet their performance remains suboptimal compared to our trained models.
These observations highlight two key limitations: 
1) general models lack domain-specific robustness to handle perturbations, and 2) existing domain models fail to fully exploit structural and numerical relationships in molecular property prediction.

Our proposed RLDBF method demonstrates significant advantages over the general and domain-specific baselines. 
It outperforms GPT-4o-mini by +20.5\% in Level 4 (In-domain) and surpasses ChemLLM by +20.3\% in the same category. 
Notably, RLDBF maintains superior consistency between In-domain and Out-domain evaluations (e.g., 47.5\% vs. 44.0\% in Level 4), indicating enhanced generalization capabilities. 
The weighted score of 109.4 further validates its balanced proficiency, exceeding the best domain model (KALE-LM-Chem: 79.7) and general models by substantial margins. 
This advancement stems from our method’s simple but efficient design, which systematically mitigates the hallucination issues observed in baseline models.

A critical ablation is observed when comparing RLDBF to its base model Llama3-8B-Instruct and other types of trained models (except for SFT Type 1 and RL Type 1, further analysis is conducted later), demonstrating that our training framework effectively amplifies the base architecture’s latent capabilities. 
The consistent outperformance across all perturbation types—particularly in Level 4 where distractors derive from structurally similar molecules—underscores the efficacy of our method.

This analysis substantiates that RLDBF establishes a new paradigm for molecular property prediction, balancing generalization and precision through its innovative training strategy. 

\paragraph{Chembench Results}

Though SFT Type 1 and RL Type 1 show impressive performance in previous experiment, as shown in Table~\ref{exp_cb}, SFT Type 1 and RL Type 1—both derived from the Llama3-8B-Instruct base model—demonstrate significant performance degradation compared to their strong results in the PubChem benchmark.
SFT Type 1 underperforms the base model in 5 out of 9 tasks, while RL Type 1 also shows declines in 5 tasks. 
This suggests that these two strategies may induce overfitting to narrow task distributions (e.g., PubChem’s property prediction), thereby compromising generalization to broader, heterogeneous tasks in ChemBench.

In contrast, our proposed RLDBF method achieves consistent improvements over the base model Llama3-8B-Instruct across multiple metrics. 
RLDBF outperforms the base model by +5.8\% in Caption2Mol, +5.3\% in Product Prediction and Retrosynthesis, and +2.2\% in overall average score. 
Furthermore, RLDBF surpasses all baseline models in Mol2Caption with a score of 93.3, demonstrating superior interpretability in molecular description generation.

Remarkably, RLDBF’s success stems solely from training on molecular property data, yet it achieves competitive performance in complex tasks like Retrosynthesis, compared to specialized models like KALE-LM. 
This demonstrates that learning fundamental molecular properties—such as hydrogen bonding patterns and partition coefficients—provides a foundational understanding that generalizes to higher-order chemical reasoning. 
For example, RLDBF’s 93.3\% accuracy in Mol2Caption suggests that property-aware training enhances the model’s ability to map structural features to semantic descriptions. 
These results highlight the untapped potential of leveraging large-scale chemical databases to augment LLMs, enabling them to transcend narrow task boundaries and achieve holistic chemical intelligence.

\paragraph{More Results}
The method’s ability to outperform cutting-edge models across challenging scenarios positions it as a potential solution for real-world cheminformatics applications.
We show some applications in appendix~\ref{app_B}(Regression) and ~\ref{app_C}(Reasoning).

\section{Conclusion}

We present RLDBF, an approach that achieves two critical objectives: 
1) enhanced utilization of high-density knowledge embedded in structured databases, 
and 2) significant reduction in human annotation costs for reinforcement learning. 
Experimental evaluations demonstrate the efficacy of our proposed method.
Looking forward, we will continue to investigate database-enhanced methodologies for LLMs, extending their applications across diverse scientific domains. 

