We systematically evaluated our methodology using a Li/Cu half-cell dataset~\cite{kim2023data} sourced from peer-reviewed chemical literature.
This comprehensive dataset encompasses multi-component electrolyte systems, each comprising 2 to 7 distinct chemical components rigorously characterized by their SMILES representations. 
To enhance the quantitative sensitivity of the model to electrolyte composition variations, we implemented a logarithmic transformation of Coulombic Efficiency (CE) according to the relationship:
\begin{equation}
LCE = -log(1 - CE).    
\end{equation}
This mathematical treatment amplifies differential responses in electrochemical performance while maintaining proportionality to the original CE metric, following established conventions for analyzing nonlinear relationships in electrochemical systems.
We compare our results with the state-of-the-art methods, including Multimodal-MoLFormer~\citep{soares2023beyond} and COEFF~\citep{li2024coeff}.

The evaluation further validates the effectiveness of our RLDBF. 
As shown in Table~\ref{exp_lce}, RLDBF+MLP achieves 0.160 RMSE, significantly outperforming the base model Llama3+MLP (0.291). Compared to SOTA methods, RLDBF demonstrates strong competitiveness. 
While COEFF-KAN achieves the lowest RMSE (0.110), our method outperforms specialized architectures like MoLFormer (0.213) and MultiModal-MoLFormer (0.195). 
Notably, RLDBF achieves this without relying on task-specific architectural modifications or extensive domain pretraining, highlighting its generalizability. 

\section{Application on reasoning tasks}
\label{app_C}

With the emergence of OpenAI-o1 and Deepseek-R1~\citep{guo2025deepseek} models, the research community has moved beyond simply requiring LLMs to provide correct answers, and now expects these models to demonstrate valid reasoning processes. 
This paradigm shift has prompted us to thoroughly investigate how to enhance model reasoning capabilities through RLDBF.

Inspired by the "Aha Moment" proposed in R1 research, we posit that enabling models to systematically compare reference answers with their current reasoning trajectory at critical cognitive checkpoints during deep thinking could effectively guide reasoning pathways and mitigate excessive rumination. 
To actualize this concept, we first distilled 500 data points on molecular physicochemical property calculations from Deepseek R1, then fine-tuned the Llama3-8B-Instruct model to cultivate both deep reasoning abilities and capacity for reference-guided reasoning (Llama3-8B Reasoner). 
Subsequently, we applied our novel RLDBF method to further reinforce the model's capability in generating accurate numerical predictions for molecular properties (RLDBF Reasoner).

The models are required to address problems through a 2-step reasoning process:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Step 1}: Directly generate the final answer (system 1, fast thinking);
    \item \textbf{Step 2}: Utilize the initial answer as a reference to perform stepwise reasoning (system 2, slow thinking).
\end{itemize}
As shown below, reasoning models examine their thoughts through recalling the reference answer, where only the correct reference leads to the correct result.

We believe this System1+System2 reasoning paradigm holds significant potential for advancing LLM applications in scientific domains. 
Our future work will focus on extending this framework to complex scenarios requiring multi-step quantitative reasoning and cross-domain knowledge integration, while further optimizing the RLDBF mechanism for improved training efficiency and reasoning robustness.

